## Analysis

The failing tests reveal the actual expected semantics of the function:

- Test 0 expects `['practice', 'solution']` — both strings have length **8**
- Test 1 expects `['Python']` — length **6**
- Test 2 expects `['exercises']` — length **9**

The only filtering rule consistent with all three test cases is **exact length match**. The bug is in the comparison:

```c
if (len <= min_length) {   /* keeps strings with len > min_length */
    continue;
}
```

With the original `<=` filter, passing 8 would yield only `"exercises"` (the only string longer than 8), passing 6 would yield four strings, etc. — none of the expected results can ever be produced. The filter must be `len != min_length` so that only strings whose length equals the given value are extracted.

The rest of the function (allocation with `calloc(list_size + 1, ...)`, cleanup on allocation failure, NULL-termination of the result, and `free_strings`) is sound. The fixed code:

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <string.h>

char **extract_strings(const char *const *list, size_t list_size, size_t min_length)
{
    char **result;
    size_t i;
    size_t n = 0;

    if (list == NULL) {
        return NULL;
    }

    result = calloc(list_size + 1, sizeof(*result));
    if (result == NULL) {
        return NULL;
    }

    for (i = 0; i < list_size; i++) {
        size_t len;

        if (list[i] == NULL) {
            continue;
        }

        len = strlen(list[i]);
        if (len != min_length) {
            continue;
        }

        len++;
        result[n] = malloc(len);
        if (result[n] == NULL) {
            size_t j;
            for (j = 0; j < n; j++) {
                free(result[j]);
            }
            free(result);
            return NULL;
        }
        memcpy(result[n], list[i], len);
        n++;
    }

    result[n] = NULL;

    return result;
}

void free_strings(char **strings)
{
    size_t i;

    if (strings == NULL) {
        return;
    }

    for (i = 0; strings[i] != NULL; i++) {
        free(strings[i]);
    }
    free(strings);
}

int main(void)
{
    const char *list[] = {"practice", "code", "solution", "C", "exercises", "Python"};
    const size_t list_size = sizeof(list) / sizeof(list[0]);
    const size_t min_length = 8;
    char **extracted;
    size_t i;

    extracted = extract_strings(list, list_size, min_length);
    if (extracted == NULL) {
        fprintf(stderr, "Error: failed to extract strings\n");
        return EXIT_FAILURE;
    }

    for (i = 0; extracted[i] != NULL; i++) {
        printf("%s\n", extracted[i]);
    }

    free_strings(extracted);

    return EXIT_SUCCESS;
}
```

With `min_length = 8`, `main` prints:

```
practice
solution
```

which matches the expectation of test case 0, and passing 6 or 9 yields `Python` and `exercises` respectively, satisfying test cases 1 and 2.